          {
            ":db/id": 17592186055582,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186055858,
              ":tutorial/content": "The graph of a linear equation will *always* be a straight line! As we know, any straight line is defined by two points.\nTherefore, instead of plotting lots of points, we only need to plot two of them, and then we can draw a straight line through them.\n\nFor example, we previously encountered a line that passed through the points $(0,-2)$, $(1,1)$, $(2,4)$, and $(3,7)$. Its graph is shown below:\n\n![](image_assets/ab2de8a70d8d7064b3d8ea0bc88ebf46-d2d8891102.png)\n\nHowever, given only two points $(0, -2)$ and $(3, 7)$, we can still draw the same line through them:\n\n![](image_assets/6025fd35ae0be7384380ad4369ab0eee-6594282ddc.png)",
              ":tutorial/id": "340a36d2-34fa-573b-b664-ce6c5bc161c9",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 1437,
              ":tutorial/title": "A Line Passing Through Two Points"
            },
            ":step/id": "3d7bd220-7cba-5605-9331-214738dd7e10",
